            </section>
            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear Colleague, I am writing to inform you regarding your patient, an 84-year-old male admitted on 25/10/2005 with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Clinical examination confirmed decreased air entry bilaterally with widespread expiratory wheeze. Chest X-ray showed pulmonary hyperinflation consistent with COPD without evidence of pneumonia or pneumothorax. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed mild hypoxaemia with compensated respiratory acidosis. Blood tests showed no significant infective markers. The patient was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and close monitoring of vital signs. He responded well to treatment with marked symptomatic improvement. He was discharged stable on his usual inhalers with a short course of oral prednisolone. Please review him in the coming weeks and ensure continuation of his COPD management plan.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
